            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your house has a significant amount of excess air leakage (heat loss) that you should make a priority to address. The current air leakage locations include:



** The heating system ductwork - the large open return air duct near the ceiling, as well as many of the floor vents. If the furnace ductwork is truly sealed through the crawlspace, the only way outside cold air could be drawn in through the ductwork would be via the chimney &amp; the 2 open combustion air supply pipes (5" up through floor &amp; 4" in through front wall). I suspect the cold air we were drawing in was coming from openings in the ductwork into the crawlspace below ........ and this should be investigated further.  As discussed, I suggest you conduct a "Garbage Bag Air Leakage" test of all of the floor registers in your house to help identify where the "breach" into the crawlspace may be. I will email you the test procedure together with this report.



** If you upgrade the furnace to a high efficiency, direct vent unit, the current chimney &amp; 2 open combustion air pipes can be eliminated. In conjunction with this, I do recommend that a dedicated outside combustion air supply pipe be directly connected to the back of your wood stove.



** Around the attic access panel (can be sealed with a removable caulking, such as Draft Attack)



** The current windows are good triple pane units, however some of their crank openers are in need of repair or replacement. Both front doors are very drafty and require a proper weatherstripping &amp; an adjustment to keep them closed tightly.



Savings: I modeled a reasonable 30% reduction in air leakage (I would hope you could achieve 50% - 60% reduction), that is estimated to save $ 869 per year in energy costs and decrease your annual energy usage by 19.9 GJ.



There are currently no rebates for air leakage reduction, unless you are participating in the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate; achieving a 20% reduction increases the rebate to $750, and a 30%+ reduction achieves the maximum rebate of $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 (R12) and have 1" (R5.0) of extruded polystyrene insulation on the exterior. We discussed an option for you to add another 3" (R15) of extruded polystyrene on the exterior - bringing the total wall nominal R-value up to R32 ....... which is what would be required for newer house construction.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). For your house, I modeled adding another R15.0, as outlined above.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R15, you could potentially save $ 540 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 12.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 5,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP),



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</mainWalls>
